  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how cryogenic CO₂ capture compares for two material types used in the process: aqueous pre-wash solvents and inert inorganic beads. A small schematic at the top left shows the basic idea of cooling a gas stream so CO₂ can be separated, emphasizing a low-temperature capture route.\n\nThe main chart rates these two options across nine performance attributes using colored circles, where blue indicates an advantage, gray is roughly neutral, yellow is a mild drawback, and red marks stronger disadvantages. Both aqueous solutions and inorganic beads show clear strengths in the first two metrics, especially CO₂ capture efficiency and regeneration energy penalty, with large blue markers. An upward arrow near the second metric suggests particularly favorable regeneration performance.\n\nFor several middle attributes—such as capacity, safety, and material degradation—the symbols are small and near gray, implying limited differentiation or mostly neutral performance. The balance shifts negative for later practical and economic criteria. Ease of handling appears somewhat unfavorable, while energy requirement, production cost, and especially operating cost are shown as disadvantages, often with pink to red circles. Overall, the figure suggests cryogenic capture offers strong technical performance but weaker cost and operational practicality.",
